{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean head d50c7f91f015d53aa60db0211788dda38c1d010d, judging artifact 3bb89b1125197fe2b01eb4cbef7ae4b14167fab0. One non-blocking finding, and one attack I expected to land that does not.\n\nThe binding is stronger than the trailers alone, which is what makes this hold. `readMergeReceipt` does not merely parse `Gitseq-Approval`, `Gitseq-Candidate` and `Gitseq-Target-Pre-Head` — it also reads `rev-list --parents` and requires exactly three fields with the first parent equal to the recorded target pre-head and the second equal to the candidate. So the receipt cannot describe a merge that did not actually merge the approved commit into the recorded target. A trailer is a claim; the parent check is what makes it evidence.\n\nThe attack I went looking for was trailer injection through `--text`, since the text is user-supplied and the trailers are appended to it. It fails, for a reason worth stating because it is not obvious from the code: the parse assigns unconditionally on each match, so the last occurrence wins, and the real trailers are always appended after the text. A crafted `--text` carrying `Gitseq-Approval: \u003csomeone-else's-approval\u003e` does make that commit match the `--grep` filter in `existingGitMergeReceipt` for the victim approval — but that function then re-parses the commit and compares `receipt.Approval == approval`, which is the last-wins value, so the crafted line is filtered out rather than trusted. The grep is a search, the parse is the authority, and they are in the right order. Confirmed by reading rather than execution.\n\nRepository-wide scope holds structurally rather than by intention. `refs/gitseq/merge-receipts/\u003chash\u003e` lives in the common ref store: from the linked worktree at request/approval-replay-receipt, `git rev-parse --git-common-dir` resolves to the main repository's .git, and `refs/gitseq/*` is visible there. A per-worktree ref namespace would have made the reservation local and the single-use claim false; this one is genuinely shared. Confirmed by execution.\n\nThe reservation is a real compare-and-swap. `update-ref \u003cref\u003e \u003ctargetPreHead\u003e \"\"` with an empty expected-old-value creates only if absent, so two callers cannot both proceed, and the deferred cleanup runs under `context.Background()` so a cancelled context still releases it. Validation runs twice, before and immediately before invoking git, and the reservation is taken after both — so a durable refusal never leaves a lock behind. `--is-ancestor` refuses a candidate already contained in the target, which closes the replay-into-the-same-branch case ahead of the receipt checks.\n\nThree records, and the third earns its place: git receipts are searched across all refs, but `log --all` only reaches reachable commits, so a deleted branch would hide the git-side evidence. The signed workroom assertion, keyed idempotently on a hash of the approval, is what survives ref or branch loss. The documentation says exactly this rather than implying the git side is sufficient.\n\nThe finding, and it is about recovery rather than correctness. A crash between reserving and merging leaves the reservation with no merge, and the next attempt is refused for ever — which is deliberate, tested by TestMergeGuardSerializesConcurrentApprovalUse, and documented as \"an interrupted reservation fails closed so it can be inspected before any later merge is allowed\". I agree with failing closed. But nothing says what to inspect or how to clear a stale reservation, and the error — \"approval is already reserved or used by another merge\" — conflates a recoverable interruption with a correctly consumed approval. An operator who hits it cannot tell which they have, and the safe test is precise and knowable: whether any commit carries `Gitseq-Approval: \u003capproval\u003e`, which is what distinguishes a used approval from an abandoned lock. Fail-closed is only safe when the way back is written down; otherwise someone invents one under pressure and deletes a ref that was doing its job. I would document the check and the recovery, and consider distinguishing the two cases in the message. Not blocking: the control is sound and the risk is operational rather than a hole.\n\nThe plain-language merge message is required rather than encouraged — a blank `--text` refuses — and the receipt trailers are appended to it rather than replacing it, so the immutable record carries a human sentence and the machine fields both.\n\nGates: `go vet ./...` clean and `go test ./cmd/gs -count=1` passes at this head; the worktree is clean. Confirmed by execution: the gates, the common-dir resolution, the head and cleanliness. Confirmed by reading only: the trailer-injection analysis and the parent-binding argument.","body":{"head":"d50c7f91f015d53aa60db0211788dda38c1d010d","verdict":"approved"}}